Title:
Terminator (Spyboy technique reproduction abusing zam64.sys)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This repository provides a Windows tool that reproduces the “Spyboy/Terminator” technique for killing EDR/XDR/AV processes by abusing a vulnerable kernel driver (zam64.sys).  
- It solves the problem of terminating protected or self-defending security products from user mode by delegating process termination/control to a kernel driver via IOCTLs.  
- The write-up highlights a driver “trusted PID” access-control mechanism that blocks untrusted callers, and shows it can be bypassed by issuing an IOCTL to add the attacker’s PID to the trusted list.  
- Operationally, the tool loads/uses `Terminator.sys` alongside the executable and must be run as Administrator; it is intended to keep running to prevent security services from restarting.  
- Useful for red teams, pentesters, and malware researchers studying BYOVD-style EDR tampering; also relevant to blue teams for understanding and detecting driver-abuse tradecraft.

Technical Focus:
- BYOVD (Bring Your Own Vulnerable Driver) for security product tampering
- Kernel driver IOCTL abuse (DeviceIoControl interface)
- “Trusted PID” / access-control bypass inside a driver
- Terminating protected/security processes from kernel context
- Windows driver/service loading and persistence during execution

Use Cases:
- Disable/kill EDR/XDR/AV processes during an engagement (lab/controlled environments)
- Research and reproduce Spyboy/Terminator tradecraft for threat emulation
- Develop detections for vulnerable-driver loading and suspicious IOCTL patterns
- Validate defensive controls (driver blocklists, HVCI, WDAC, EDR anti-tamper)

Keywords:
Terminator, Spyboy, zam64.sys, BYOVD, vulnerable driver, Windows kernel driver, IOCTL, DeviceIoControl, EDR bypass, AV killer, XDR, protected process termination, trusted PID, access control bypass, loldrivers, driver loading, anti-tamper, Windows services, Terminator.sys, GMER driver (related context)